# Frontend Guidelines

React/TypeScript-specific practices. Apply when writing or reviewing `.ts` and `.tsx` files.

## GUIDE-01 — No Inline Handler Definitions in JSX

Extract handlers into named `useCallback` or `useMemo` declarations. Inline arrow functions in JSX are harder to read, test, and optimise.

```tsx
// ❌
<QuestionBank onDrop={insertIndex => {
  setIndex(insertIndex);
  setModals(prev => ({ ...prev, questionBank: true }));
}} />

// ✅
const handleQuestionBankDrop = useCallback((insertIndex: number) => {
  setIndex(insertIndex);
  setModals(prev => ({ ...prev, questionBank: true }));
}, []);

<QuestionBank onDrop={handleQuestionBankDrop} />
```

## GUIDE-02 — Hook Declaration Order

> **applyWhen:** Writing or reviewing React component functions that declare hooks.

Declare hooks in the following order within every component:

1. All other hooks (context, refs, router, etc.)
2. `useState`
3. `useMemo`
4. `useCallback`
5. `useEffect`

## GUIDE-03 — Prefer Computed Values and useMemo for Derived State

Derived state should be computed, not synced. Use `useMemo` for values derived from props or state. Reserve `useEffect` for side effects that synchronise with external systems (DOM, subscriptions, timers, APIs).

```tsx
// ❌ — effect to mirror prop into state
useEffect(() => setLocalValue(propValue), [propValue]);

// ✅ — derive with useMemo or use prop directly
const displayValue = useMemo(() => propValue ?? defaultValue, [propValue]);
```

## GUIDE-04 — Pass Source Values Instead of Derived Booleans

When a component needs to branch on a value, pass the source value rather than a pre-derived boolean. The consumer can derive booleans locally. Passing the source keeps the contract explicit and allows branching on future variants without new props.

```tsx
// ❌ — derived boolean; loses information
<QuestionBankSection isSurvey={formType === 'survey'} />

// ✅ — pass source value
<QuestionBankSection formType={formType} />
```

## GUIDE-05 — Prefer Inferred Props Over `React.FC`

Define component props on the function parameter instead of typing the component as `React.FC`. This keeps prop contracts explicit and avoids implicit `children` behavior.

```tsx
// ❌
const Component: React.FC<Props> = ({ title, onClose }) => {
  return <Modal title={title} onClose={onClose} />;
};

// ✅
const Component = ({ title, onClose }: Props) => {
  return <Modal title={title} onClose={onClose} />;
};
```
